  "verbalization": "Calcitriol, through its structural resemblance to dihydrotachysterol and alfacalcidol, shares a common pathway with vitamin D analogs, which are known to bind to the vitamin D receptor (VDR) and modulate gene expression. This structural similarity suggests a potential for Calcitriol to interact with the VDR, potentially influencing downstream signaling pathways. The binding of alfacalcidol to Gc, a protein involved in vitamin D metabolism, indicates a possible mechanism by which Calcitriol might exert its effects on Gc. The shared ancestry of Calcitriol and alfacalcidol with vitamin D analogs further supports the notion that Calcitriol could act through a similar mechanism as other vitamin D derivatives. The evidence suggests that Calcitriol may interact with Gc via a pathway involving vitamin D receptor-mediated signaling, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
